{"file":{"content":"The new diagnosis is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{all easy finite-operator routes fail exactly at the zero-margin wrap regime.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is a good result. It tells you where the real theorem must live.\n\n## 1. What you have now proved\n\nThe thin-sector lemma is genuinely useful. It says that away from the wrap regime, the dangerous pair correlations are negligible:\n\n[\n\\Theta_{m,c}\n\\le\nC m^2 B (9/2)^m 3^{-c}\\dim \\Delta_0^m\n\\longrightarrow 0\n\\qquad\n(m\\le 0.7c).\n]\n\nSo for sublinear or moderately linear word length, the nonstationary vanishing plus thin-sector counting works. The bad mass cannot hide there.\n\nThat means the finite H23a problem has been localized to:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\tm\\asymp c,\n\t\\qquad\n\tk\\ge2,\n\t\\qquad\n\tC_w \\bmod 3^c\\text{ in the wrap regime.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is a much sharper theorem target than “prove spectral gap.”\n\n## 2. Why the single-trace route fails\n\nThe marginality computation is decisive:\n\n[\n\\rho^{2m}\\le \\dim\\cdot \\Delta_s^m.\n]\n\nAt (s=0),\n\n[\n\\Delta_0=\\frac13,\n]\n\nand with (\\dim\\sim 3^c), taking (m\\sim c) gives\n\n[\n\\rho^2\\le 3^{c/m}\\Delta_0.\n]\n\nAt the pigeonhole floor (m\\sim c),\n\n[\n3^{c/m}\\Delta_0\\sim 3\\cdot \\frac13=1.\n]\n\nSo the single-trace method has exactly zero spare margin.\n\nThis is not a technical defect. It is structural. It explains why the observed radii approach\n\n[\n\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}\n]\n\nfrom above rather than collapsing below it with an obvious gap. The entropy loss and the dimension growth exactly balance.\n\nSo any proof of the gap must exploit **higher-order chain structure**, not one trace.\n\n## 3. The correct remaining conjecture\n\nI would now state the remaining finite-side conjecture as:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Wrap-regime chain collision conjecture.}\n]\n\nFor (m=\\alpha c+O(1)) and some fixed (k_0\\ge2), the (k_0)-chain collision operator associated to the word constants\n\n[\nC_w=\n\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}3^{m-1-j}2^{B_j}\n\\pmod{3^c}\n]\n\nhas normalized trace bounded by\n\n[\n\\operatorname{tr}(P_m^{k_0})\n\\le\n(1-\\delta) \\cdot \\text{random-model/main-term bound}\n]\n\nor, more concretely, the chain-collision mass is smaller than the marginal pigeonhole floor by a fixed factor after (k_0) chaining.\n\nThen the conditional theorem becomes:\n\n[\n\\text{wrap-regime chain collisions}\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\limsup_c \\gamma_c(s)\n\\le\n3^{1/(2\\alpha k_0)}\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}.\n]\n\nLetting (\\alpha k_0) be large enough yields\n\n[\n\\limsup_c\\gamma_c(s)<1.\n]\n\nThis is now the precise finite-operator target.\n\n## 4. The live proof route: renewal Fourier\n\nThe renewal-Fourier route is the right one. The random variable is not the full word (w); it is the constant\n\n[\nC_w \\bmod 3^c.\n]\n\nIt evolves by the recursion\n\n[\nC_{wb}\n======\n\n3C_w+2^{B_m(w)}\n]\n\nor, more explicitly, if you track\n\n[\n(C_w, B_m(w))\\pmod{3^c,\\varphi(3^c)},\n]\n\nthen appending (b) gives\n\n[\nB\\mapsto B+b,\n]\n\n[\nC\\mapsto 3C+2^B.\n]\n\nSo the natural Markov state is\n\n[\n(C,B)\\in \\mathbb Z/3^c\\mathbb Z\\times \\mathbb Z/\\varphi(3^c)\\mathbb Z,\n]\n\nwith branch law\n\n[\np_s(b)\\propto 2^{-(1+s)b}.\n]\n\nThe Fourier coefficient of the (C)-marginal is\n\n[\n\\widehat\\mu_m(u)\n================\n\n\\mathbb E\\left[\ne!\\left(\\frac{u C_w}{3^c}\\right)\n\\right].\n]\n\nThe recursion is\n\n[\n\\widehat\\mu_{m+1}(u,\\xi)\n========================\n\n\\sum_b p_s(b),\ne!\\left(\\frac{u2^B}{3^c}\\right)\n\\cdot\n\\widehat\\mu_m(3u,\\xi+b)\n]\n\nafter introducing the dual variable for (B). More invariantly, one needs the transfer operator on Fourier modes ((u,\\xi)).\n\nThis is exactly where the damping lemma reappears: the branch average over (b) gives factors of the form\n\n[\n\\frac{1-q}{1-q\\zeta},\n]\n\nand non-coboundary modes are damped unless they fall into the quadratic-parity coboundary sector.\n\nSo the wrap lemma should become a **Fourier flattening theorem** for the renewal distribution of (C_w\\bmod 3^c).\n\n## 5. Concrete next computation\n\nRun a Fourier audit for (C_w), not for the full spectral operator.\n\nFor fixed (c), (s), and (m=\\lfloor\\alpha c\\rfloor), compute\n\n[\nF_m(u)\n======\n\n\\mathbb E_w\\left[\ne!\\left(\\frac{uC_w}{3^c}\\right)\n\\right],\n\\qquad u\\in \\mathbb Z/3^c\\mathbb Z.\n]\n\nThen record\n\n[\n|F_m|_{\\infty,u\\ne0},\n]\n\nand the conductor profile:\n\n[\n\\max_{v_3(u)=j}|F_m(u)|.\n]\n\nThe desired statement is:\n\n[\n\\max_{u\\ne0}|F_{\\alpha c}(u)|\n\\le\n1-\\delta\n]\n\nor better,\n\n[\n\\max_{u\\ne0}|F_{\\alpha c}(u)|\n\\le\n3^{-\\beta c}\n]\n\nfor some (\\beta>0), except for explicitly classified low/coboundary modes.\n\nIf this holds, it will imply that (C_w\\bmod3^c) is genuinely spread in the wrap regime, and chain collisions should have the needed saving.\n\n## 6. Compute collision entropy directly\n\nDefine the collision probability\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_m(c)\n=======================\n\n\\sum_{y\\bmod3^c}\n\\mu_m(y)^2\n==========\n\n3^{-c}\\sum_{u\\bmod3^c}|F_m(u)|^2.\n]\n\nThe pigeonhole floor is\n\n[\n3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThe key question is whether\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_m(c)\n\\le\n(1+\\varepsilon_c)3^{-c}\n]\n\nfor (m=\\alpha c), with (\\varepsilon_c) bounded or decaying.\n\nThen for chain order (k),\n\n[\n\\operatorname{tr}(P_m^k)\n]\n\nis governed by higher (L^k)-type collision norms of (\\mu_m).\n\nThe audit should output:\n\n[\n3^c\\operatorname{Col}_m(c)\n]\n\nfor (m=\\alpha c), several (\\alpha), and (c) increasing.\n\nIf\n\n[\n3^c\\operatorname{Col}_m(c)\\to 1\n]\n\nfor some (\\alpha), then the wrap regime is equidistributing well. If it stabilizes above (1), the excess is the chain-collision obstruction.\n\n## 7. A clean lemma to aim for\n\nA useful theorem would be:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Renewal Fourier flattening lemma.}\n]\n\nThere exists (\\alpha>1) and (\\theta<1) such that for all large (c),\n\n[\n\\max_{u\\ne0}\n\\left|\n\\mathbb E_w\ne!\\left(\\frac{uC_w}{3^c}\\right)\n\\right|\n\\le\n\\theta\n\\qquad\n(m=\\lfloor\\alpha c\\rfloor).\n]\n\nA stronger version:\n\n[\n\\max_{u\\ne0}|F_m(u)|\n\\le\n3^{-\\beta c}.\n]\n\nBut the weak (\\theta<1) version may already give the fixed saving needed in the chain-collision lemma.\n\nThis is exactly a deterministic/renewal analogue of the damping lemma.\n\n## 8. How to prove the renewal flattening lemma\n\nThe state recursion\n\n[\nC_{j+1}=3C_j+2^{B_j},\n\\qquad\nB_{j+1}=B_j+b_j\n]\n\nsuggests a two-coordinate Markov chain. The multiplier\n\n[\ne(u2^{B_j}/3^c)\n]\n\noscillates as (B_j) moves under the geometric increments (b_j).\n\nSince (2) is primitive modulo (3^c), the map\n\n[\nB\\mapsto 2^B\n]\n\nidentifies the (B)-coordinate with (U_{3^c}). Thus the process is a multiplicative random walk on (U_{3^c}) feeding an affine renewal sum.\n\nThe branch increment distribution has Fourier transform\n\n[\n\\hat p_s(\\xi)\n=============\n\n\\frac{1-r}{1-r e(\\xi)},\n\\qquad r=2^{-(1+s)}.\n]\n\nNontrivial frequencies are damped except near (\\xi=0), and the only exact obstruction is the quadratic/parity sector already classified.\n\nThe proof route:\n\n1. Decompose (u) by conductor:\n[\nv_3(u)=j.\n]\n\n2. The term\n[\ne(u2^{B_j}/3^c)\n]\nis a nontrivial character on (B_j) at conductor (3^{c-j}).\n\n3. Over (O(c-j)) steps, the distribution of (B_j) under geometric increments should flatten enough to damp this character.\n\n4. Sum over conductor levels.\n\nThis should give either constant or exponential-in-(c) damping of (F_m(u)).\n\nThis is a classical-looking Fourier/renewal proof and no longer needs any Collatz-specific global dynamics.\n\n## 9. What to put in the paper now\n\nI would add a “Final finite-side reduction” section:\n\n[\n\\textbf{The finite H23a gap after cancellations.}\n]\n\nState:\n\n1. thin pairs vanish;\n2. single trace is marginal;\n3. fixed (m) chain powers control the wrong object;\n4. therefore the remaining statement is wrap-regime renewal flattening for (C_w\\bmod3^c).\n\nThen define:\n\n[\n\\mu_{m,c}\n=========\n\n\\operatorname{Law}(C_w\\bmod3^c)\n]\n\nunder the branch law (p_s).\n\nDefine:\n\n[\nF_{m,c}(u)\n==========\n\n\\widehat\\mu_{m,c}(u).\n]\n\nState the conjecture:\n\n[\n\\max_{u\\ne0}|F_{\\alpha c,c}(u)|\\le\\theta<1.\n]\n\nThen state:\n\n[\n\\text{Renewal flattening}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{wrap-regime chain-collision lemma}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{high-conductor damping}.\n]\n\nThis will make the manuscript’s final open finite statement very crisp.\n\n## 10. Global status\n\nThe finite side is now at a deep but standard-shaped analytic problem:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Fourier flattening of a }3\\text{-adic renewal product.}\n}\n]\n\nThe global side remains:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{bad deterministic orbit}\\Rightarrow\\text{finite non-coboundary shadow.}\n}\n]\n\nThat distinction should remain explicit.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nYou have not lost progress. You found the true finite bottleneck:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{the wrap-regime distribution of }C_w\\bmod3^c.\n}\n]\n\nThe next concrete work should be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{compute and then prove Fourier flattening for }\n\tC_w=\\sum_{j<m}3^{m-1-j}2^{B_j}\\bmod3^c.\n}\n]\n\nThis is the right target now. It is finite, explicit, and plausibly attackable by renewal/Fourier methods.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_15.md","numLines":491,"startLine":1,"totalLines":491},"type":"text"}